                    "Now the comparison, and the instruments are different so read it carefully.  The same night on television, in Nielsen's preliminary ratings: NBC News <mark>6.52 million</mark>, ABC News 4.91 million, CNN 3.93 million, MS NOW 3.92 million, Fox News 3.88 million, CBS News 2.63 million, a six-network total of <mark>25.79 million</mark> against 25.43 million cited for 2022.  Those are average viewers across the evening.  The 3.4 million from streams is a peak, which is the softer measure, so the honest statement is that the whole of streaming at its single best minute was smaller than one television network's evening average.  And the television figures exclude out-of-home viewing and Nielsen's big-data component, so they will move.",
                    "The inflation example is in the same report and it is unusually clean.  <mark>NBC News took 4.39 million hours watched, 21 percent of everything across all four platforms, from three simultaneous broadcasts whose peaks together came to above 667,000.</mark>  Three channels carrying overlapping coverage count their hours three times and their peak once.  That is the whole problem with hours watched in one line, and it comes from a news organisation rather than from anyone trying to game a sponsorship.",
